---
const steps = [
	{ who: 'you', what: 'scroll, watch, search, buy, walk around with a phone' },
	{ who: 'devices + apps', what: 'TVs, phones and free apps log it, tied to an advertising ID' },
	{ who: 'SDKs + pixels', what: 'tracking code inside apps and pages ships it out, often to dozens of companies' },
	{ who: 'data brokers', what: 'merge it with purchase records, location and public records into a profile' },
	{ who: 'ad exchange', what: 'auctions your attention in ~100 ms, every time a page or app loads' },
	{ who: 'advertisers', what: 'pay for whatever reaction is most likely, and the feed learns to provoke it' },
];
---

<section id="machine">
	<h2>the machine</h2>
	<p class="lede">
		No person is sitting there watching you. Nobody needs to. It is a pipeline that runs on its
		own, and every stage is paid by the next one.
	</p>
	<ol class="pipe">
		{steps.map((s, i) => (
			<li style={`--i:${i}`}>
				<span class="who">{s.who}</span>
				<span class="what">{s.what}</span>
			</li>
		))}
	</ol>
	<p class="note">
		The loop closes at the bottom: whatever gets a reaction gets shown more, so the system drifts
		toward things that wind people up. Nobody has to intend that for it to happen. The fix is the
		same either way: stop feeding it, which is the next section.
	</p>
</section>

<style>
	.pipe { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; counter-reset: step; }
	.pipe li {
		position: relative;
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr;
		gap: 16px;
		padding: 12px 14px;
		background: var(--panel);
		border: 1px solid var(--line);
		border-left: 3px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--green), var(--red) calc(var(--i) * 20%));
		border-radius: 4px;
		margin-bottom: 22px;
	}
	.pipe li:not(:last-child)::after {
		content: '↓';
		position: absolute;
		left: 20px;
		bottom: -22px;
		color: var(--dim);
	}
	.who { color: var(--green); }
	.what { font-size: 0.92rem; }
	.note { color: var(--dim); max-width: 64ch; }
	@media (max-width: 560px) {
		.pipe li { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 2px; }
	}
</style>
